---
import DocsPage from '../../components/DocsPage.astro'
import { sourceUrl } from '../../data/source-projection'
import { sitePath } from '../../data/site-path'
---

<DocsPage
  title="Vue adapter source"
  eyebrow="PRE-RELEASE FRAMEWORK REFERENCE"
  summary="Render a caller-owned projection in a Vue component without creating a second spreadsheet store."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/docs/vue/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-warning" aria-label="Vue adapter status">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">STATUS: REPOSITORY SOURCE ONLY</p>
      <p>
        <code>@einfach/vue-excel</code> is a private workspace package at version <code>0.0.0</code>. It is not published to
        npm, has no default Worker or WASM backend, and is not a complete spreadsheet application. Treat this as source to
        evaluate, not as a package to install.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>1. Install the repository workspace</h2>
    <p>The Vue adapter is consumed from this repository today, so install the workspace once before running its fixture.</p>
    <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/allroad88888888/einfach-excel.git
cd einfach-excel
pnpm install`}</code></pre>

    <h2>2. Run the verified Vue fixture</h2>
    <pre><code>{`NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1 \
pnpm --filter @einfach/vue-excel run e2e`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      This command starts the repository's Vite fixture, opens Chromium, drags across controlled cells, and verifies the
      projected selection. A successful run reports one passing Vue adapter test.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Understand the available boundary</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-terms">
      <article><strong>Controlled grid</strong><span>Bind caller-owned cells and a finite range to a Vue grid view.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Provider hooks</strong><span>Bridge UI-core selection, navigation, editing, clipboard, and history commands.</span></article>
      <article><strong>One source of truth</strong><span>Vue reactivity manages subscriptions and cleanup; spreadsheet state remains in UI-core atoms.</span></article>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Compose one controlled projection</h2>
    <p>This is a repository-source pattern. The Vue host owns the backend, cells, and range; the adapter only renders that projection.</p>
    <pre><code>{`<script setup lang="ts">
import type {
  CellRange,
  DisplayCell,
  SpreadsheetBackend,
} from '@einfach/spreadsheet-ui-core'
import {
  SpreadsheetGridView,
  SpreadsheetUiProvider,
} from '@einfach/vue-excel'

defineProps<{
  backend: SpreadsheetBackend
  cells: readonly DisplayCell[]
  range: CellRange
}>()
</script>

<template>
  <SpreadsheetUiProvider :backend="backend">
    <SpreadsheetGridView :cells="cells" :range="range" />
  </SpreadsheetUiProvider>
</template>`}</code></pre>

    <h2>5. Keep these responsibilities in the host</h2>
    <ol class="docs-guide-steps">
      <li>Keep the supplied backend stable while one workbook is mounted; a replacement intentionally creates a new UI core.</li>
      <li>Own the data query and pass the returned finite <code>cells</code> and <code>range</code> into the view.</li>
      <li>Use adapter hooks for interactions. Do not mirror spreadsheet product state into a second set of Vue refs.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>6. Inspect the interactive reference</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('demos/vue-controlled-projection/')}><strong>Run the Vue projection demo</strong><span>Drag across its fixed 4×4 grid and inspect the selected range. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/excel-site/src/islands/VueAdapterDemoIsland.vue')}><strong>Read the exact host source</strong><span>It supplies the provider, deterministic backend, store, projection, and pointer selection. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/vue-excel/README.md')}><strong>Read the package boundary</strong><span>See the supported primitives and the deliberate non-goals. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>What is not ready</h2>
    <p>
      There is no published Vue release, default Worker factory, WASM packaging, browser end-to-end promise, or turnkey shell.
      For a production integration today, use the supported <a href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/')}>Solid path</a> or
      implement the <a href={sitePath('docs/backend-port/')}>backend port</a> while the Vue adapter matures.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
